<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p><h1 class="research-title">42 BCE</h1><p class="ref-lede">42 BCE in world history: 3 events, 1 birth and 2 deaths. 174 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/49-bce">49 BCE</a><a href="/years/46-bce">46 BCE</a><a href="/years/44-bce">44 BCE</a><a href="/years/43-bce">43 BCE</a><a href="/years/42-bce" aria-current="page">42 BCE</a><a href="/years/31-bce">31 BCE</a><a href="/years/30-bce">30 BCE</a><a href="/years/27-bce">27 BCE</a><a href="/years/23">23 CE</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-caesar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Roman Senate deifies the murdered Julius Caesar</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Two years after his assassination, the Senate declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> a god of the Roman state as Divus Iulius. The honour let his adopted heir Octavian, the future Augustus, style himself 'son of a god'.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-3">3 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Antony and Octavian fight Brutus and Cassius at Philippi</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In the first battle of Philippi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> defeated Cassius and took his camp, while Brutus overran Octavian's troops. The battle was a tactical draw, but Cassius, believing it lost, took his own life to avoid capture.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Battle of Philippi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-cassius-longinus">Gaius Cassius Longinus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Brutus takes his own life after defeat at Philippi</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and Octavian in the second engagement at Philippi in Macedonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a>, a leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s assassins, took his own life. The triumvirs' victory ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">the Liberators</a>' civil war and the republican cause.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-philippi">Battle of Philippi</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-junius-brutus">Marcus Junius Brutus</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberators-civil-war">Liberators' civil war</a></div></div></li></ol></section>
